172	0.3	14	NewsDATA	Revised SHS program a step in the right direction	THE senior high school program, which is part of the K-12 program, aimed at bringing our public school system on par with the rest of the world in the number of years spent schooling by Filipino students. It was a good idea that was rushed by the Aquino administration.The books were hardly there and updated training workshops were spotty. The Duterte administration tried to add many other subjects to the K-12 curriculum, overloading it with learning material beyond the basics. The result was half-baked education for most, as proven by Filipino students' dismal ratings in global assessments in reading, mathematics and science.Now comes the good news that the K-12 curriculum would be overhauled, with a focus on senior high school (SHS) first. The full execution of the strengthened senior high school program will be done next year, the Department of Education (DepEd) announced last Friday. The pilot testing will be conducted in 841 schools this year, with 580 public and 261 private schools participating, Education Undersecretary Gina Gonong said. Thirty-five of the pilot schools selected are in rural areas.The changes to the SHS program include a decongested curriculum and more work-immersion hours in the form of internships in companies. The bane of an overloaded curriculum has been solved. The core subjects will be decreased from 15 to five: Effective Communication, Life Skills, General Mathematics, General Science and Pag-aaral ng Kasaysayan at Lipunang Pilipino (Study of History and Filipino Society).Moreover, the revised curriculum recognized the need for electives so that the students could take the subjects that would enrich their learning or prepare them for the world of work. Two elective subjects will be available for Grade 11 students, while Grade 12 learners have five to seven electives.The students' work immersion hours will also be increased to 640 from 340. "This is one of the challenges mentioned by our industry partners. They said students need to be prepared to get hired," Gonong said. The DepEd will ask the help of the National Educators Academy of the Philippines to train teachers covered by the pilot implementation.Gonong said that the agency will also implement the academic recovery and acceleration program this school year. "Congress has approved the learning recovery program, where students who encounter problems in their studies will be tutored so that we can achieve grade-level competency, including functional literacy." In this space three weeks ago, we also lauded the Commission on Higher Education (CHED) for their innovative project. They are hiring junior and senior college students in state colleges and universities to give tutorials to poor public elementary school students who cannot read. The benefits are twofold: the college students augment their funds for school needs, and the elementary students (with the aid of their parents) learn how to read.There are around 2,739 senior high schools operating in the country. The upcoming school year is set to open on June 16 and end on March 31, 2026. Therefore, there is still enough time to download the learning materials and train the teachers for the implementation of this important project.Shortage of teachersThis space has applauded the hiring of 16,000 new teachers. However, we suggested that the hiring be focused on the poorest provinces, most of which are in Mindanao. These are also areas with weak internet, which undercuts the students' learning. Some schools in far-flung areas use the hybrid mode but cannot do so because of weak Wi-Fi.However, the actual figures of teacher shortage in the country are higher than the DepEd's estimate of 30,000. That is, if we follow the international standard ratio of one teacher to 35 students, according to the Teachers' Dignity Coalition (TDC).About 16,000 new teaching positions this year were indeed approved by the budget department, said TDC chairman Benjo Basas. "But that is not enough. The shortage is still big," he noted.The Philippines may have an actual shortage of 150,000 teachers, Alliance of Concerned Teachers national chairman Vladimer Quetua said. Unlike top-performing schools worldwide, Quetua noted that the country has a teacher-to-student ratio of "one to 45 or even 55."In another development, the DBM has approved 10,000 new non-teaching positions this year, covering administrative officer II posts with salary grade 11, or a monthly pay of about P27,000.DepEd has done well with phase 1 of its work. It can move to phase 2 in the coming year.	ONLY AVAILABLE IN PAID PLANS	https://www.manilatimes.net/2025/05/27/opinion/editorial/revised-shs-program-a-step-in-the-right-direction/2121047	https://www.manilatimes.net/manilatimes/uploads/images/2025/05/26/637118.jpg	2025-05-26 16:10:00
