193	0.343	15	NewsDATA	Circumcisions beyond control	AT first, it seemed like a story of little consequence — a footnote in the daily grind of urban tragedy. A 10-year-old boy died after a botched circumcision in an unlicensed clinic in Tondo, Manila. The loss was sudden, senseless and devastating. Watching the grief of his family unfold was a painful reminder of the human cost behind what might otherwise be dismissed as a statistic.For many Filipinos, circumcision — or "tuli" — is a deeply rooted cultural rite of passage. It is not merely a medical procedure but a social ritual that marks the transition from boyhood to manhood.Generations of Filipino males have undergone it, often during summer breaks, in the company of peers.For many, it's a shared memory — sometimes of pride, sometimes of pain, but almost always of necessity. A boy who remains uncircumcised past a certain age may be ridiculed or ostracized, regardless of reason.But what happens when a cultural tradition becomes so normalized that its dangers are ignored?The recent tragedy in Tondo, wherein a 10-year-old boy died under the "care" of an unlicensed doctor, raises hard questions about how we continue to practice and perceive circumcision in the Philippines. Despite advances in medical science and accessibility to safe procedures, thousands of boys still undergo tuli in makeshift clinics, public parks, or under the hands of unlicensed practitioners, often with no anesthesia, sterile tools, or proper aftercare.There is a romanticized notion of the "traditional" circumcision — quick, cheap, and done by hilots or local healers who have performed the procedure for decades. The pain the boys undergo is treated as nothing more than part of the process. But this approach, often praised for its cultural authenticity, poses real dangers: infection, excessive bleeding, mutilation, and in rare cases, death. In the Tondo case, the absence of emergency medical support turned a routine ritual into a fatal error.It's time we reconcile tradition with responsibility, or else risk seeing this case happen repeatedly.The Department of Health frequently carries out mass circumcision campaigns, often through outreach programs. While these initiatives are more medically supervised, they still highlight the ongoing pressure to conform to local beliefs. We need to ensure that these procedures are not only safe but also voluntary and informed. That means educating both parents and children about the risks involved and recognizing that cultural expectations should never override a child's right to health and safety.Moreover, stricter enforcement against illegal clinics and unlicensed practitioners is long overdue. No parent should be lulled into a false sense of security because a neighbor or community elder has been "doing this for years." Tradition does not equate with expertise. Familiarity should not excuse negligence.This is not a call to abandon a longstanding Filipino practice. It is a call to bring it into the 21st century — to uphold its meaning while ensuring its safety. Culture must evolve with knowledge and compassion. Otherwise, we risk more than just pain; we risk lives.In the end, the boy from Tondo did not die because of culture. He died because we failed to safeguard it with responsibility and reason.I grew up in Fabie, a depressed community behind the Paco railroad station. In our neighborhood, the rite of tuli was both anticipated and feared — a defining ritual of boyhood. For many, the man of the hour was Mang Tomas, a part-time cook who also slaughtered and roasted pigs. During summers, he became the go-to circumciser for boys who felt ready to "become men."Mang Tomas, like many others in similar communities, was not medically trained. His tools were basic. His technique, unorthodox. The boys were asked to chew guava leaves and spit the pulp onto the cut foreskin — a traditional method believed to aid healing. But it often led to infections, swelling, and days of unnecessary suffering. My father, a public school teacher, was not impressed. He knew better. And so, in our case, we waited — for doctors, for safety, for sense to prevail over pressure.That memory lingers as I reflect on what happened in Tondo. Decades may have passed, but the tension between tradition and proper medical care remains. The fact that communities still rely on unlicensed circumcisers speaks to broader issues: lack of access to health care, persistent poverty, and the slow pace of public education when it comes to medical safety.We have long accepted the pain of tuli as a test of character — a rite not only of passage but of endurance. But the real test now is for our society. Can we modernize our practices without losing their meaning? Can we ensure safety without shaming tradition? Can we protect our children without breaking the cultural bonds that shape our identity?The answers are not simple. But what is clear is that no child should die from a ritual meant to mark a new beginning.We need to act — not to erase tradition, but to protect those who live it. The Department of Health must strengthen its oversight of all medical procedures, including culturally embedded ones like circumcision. Local governments should be more proactive in offering free, safe, and well-supervised alternatives, especially in underprivileged communities. Schools and barangays can play a vital role in educating families about the risks of informal practices. And parents — especially fathers, like mine once did — must have the courage to ask hard questions and make informed choices, even when tradition urges otherwise.Our culture should never be a reason to endanger life. If anything, it should be a reason to care more, do better, and demand safety for every child. The boy from Tondo deserved that. So do all our sons."There can be no keener revelation of a society's soul than the way in which it treats its children," said Nelson Mandela.	ONLY AVAILABLE IN PAID PLANS	https://www.manilatimes.net/2025/05/27/opinion/columns/circumcisions-beyond-control/2121036	https://www.manilatimes.net/manilatimes/uploads/images/2025/05/26/637106.jpg	2025-05-26 16:08:00
